<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Privy Council of Ireland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Privy Council of Ireland was the body of senior officials and nobles that advised the lord deputy or lord lieutenant in governing Ireland. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poynings-law">Poynings' Law</a> it shared with the English council control over which bills could be put before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a>. It survived the Act of Union and was abolished after the creation of the Irish Free State in 1922.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The council grew in the Lordship of Ireland on the model of the Privy Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>: as the English council advised the king in person, the Irish council advised his viceroy. Like the English body it emerged from the medieval king's council, beside the great council, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a>, and the larger afforced council. In the early modern period it gained influence at the viceroy's expense, and it directed much of the Tudor conquest of Ireland, setting up presidencies in Munster and Connaught.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poynings-law">Poynings' Law</a> of 1495 it drafted the bills for each new parliament, which the Commons and Lords could pass or reject but not amend. Between parliaments it issued Acts of State, and from 1571 to 1641 it sat as the Court of Castle Chamber in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Members were sworn by letters patent at a council meeting. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> they included the chancellor, the treasurer, senior judges and the Church of Ireland Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Meath; later they were mostly peers, then more members of the Commons, and by the 18th century there were over 100, few of whom attended. The viceroy consulted an inner circle in advance, but Ireland's lack of responsible government kept it from becoming a cabinet. Catholics were barred until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-catholic-relief-act-1829">Roman Catholic Relief Act 1829</a>; the next Catholic member was Anthony Richard Blake in 1836.</p><p>It met in the Council Chamber of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, moving to the old Custom House on Essex Quay in 1655 and returning after a fire in 1711 destroyed its chamber and archives. Its members were styled Right Honourable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The council lost ground to the Irish parliament in the 18th century; the Patriot Party defeated the Privy Council Money Bill in 1768, and the Constitution of 1782 removed the council from law-making. Kept after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1800">Acts of Union 1800</a>, its executive role became formal and ceremonial. Its committees heard appeals under various 19th-century acts.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-ireland-act-1920">Government of Ireland Act 1920</a> retained it for the whole island. On 16 January 1922 Viscount FitzAlan handed over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a> administration to the Provisional Government in its Council Chamber, though the council itself did not meet for the occasion. Its last Order in Council was made on 5 December 1922; from the next day a Privy Council of Northern Ireland took over its work there, and the Irish council did not meet again. Its last surviving member, Hugh O'Neill, 1st Baron Rathcavan, died on 28 November 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Privy Council of Ireland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The king was represented in Dublin Castle by a lord deputy, later styled lord lieutenant, assisted by the <b>Irish Privy Council</b> and senior officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
